ected value $\\textrm{E}\\big[Y \\,|\\, X=x\\big]$ for $0 <     x <     2.$"] [#edb/ref 17592186162299 "Let $D=[0,2]\\times [0,2].$ Which of the following functions are valid joint probability density functions?\n\nI. $f(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}x+y,\\, & (x,y)\\in D \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}$\n\nII. $g(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}-xy,\\, & (x,y)\\in D \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}$\n\nIII. $h(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4}xy,\\, & (x,y)\\in D \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}$"] [#edb/ref 17592186162301 "The random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}1,\\, & 0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind $P(X>Y).$"] [#edb/ref 17592186162302 "The random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}4xy,\\, & 0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind $P(X>\\frac{1}{2},Y>\\frac{1}{2}).$"] [#edb/ref 17592186162313 "Find $\\text{E}[X{Y}^{2}],$ given that random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}1,\\, & 0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186162314 "Find $\\text{E}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(X+Y)],$ given that random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{8}{{\\pi }^{2}},\\, & 0\\le x\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le y\\le x, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186162390 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with the following joint probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}1, & \\,0<x<1,\\,x-1<y<1-x \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the expression of the conditional probability density function ${f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)$ for $0<x<1,\\,x-1<y<1-x.$"] [#edb/ref 17592186162404 "Find $\\text{E}[X{Y}^{3}],$ given that random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{9{y}^{2}},\\, & 0\\le x\\le {y}^{3},\\,0\\le y\\le 3; \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186162417 "$$\n{f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,2)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{4}, & \\,1\\le x\\le 3 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $X$ and $Y$ be continuous random variables. \nFind the conditional expected value $\\text{E}[X\\,|\\,Y=2]$ given that the conditional probability density function ${f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,2)$ is shown above."] [#edb/ref 17592186162425 "Suppose we have a population of size $N=20,$ where $55%$ of the population has a particular characteristic. Let the random variable ${X}_{i}$ be equal to\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=\\{\\begin{aligned}1,\\,i\\text{th member of the population has the characteristic} \\\\ 0,\\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor $i=1,…,20.$ Find $P({X}_{2}=1|{X}_{1}=1).$"] [#edb/ref 17592186162429 "The random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-x-y},\\, & x>0,\\,y>0, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the covariance of $X$ and $Y.$"] [#edb/ref 17592186162433 "Suppose we have a random sample of size $n=20$ from a population where $55%$ of the population has a particular characteristic. Let the random variable ${X}_{i}$ be equal to\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=\\{\\begin{aligned}1,\\,i\\text{th member of the sample has the characteristic} \\\\ 0,\\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $i=1,…,20.$ If the statistic $X$ is defined as\n\n$$\nX={\\sum }_{i=1}^{20}{X}_{i}\n$$\n\nfind approximate values of $\\text{E}[X]$ and $\\text{Var}[X].$\n\n*Hint: You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.*"] [#edb/ref 17592186162641 "Find the interquartile range of the random variable $X$ if the cumulative distribution function of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0,\\, & x<1, \\\\ 1-{e}^{1-x},\\, & x\\ge 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186162644 "Find the $30$th percentile of the random variable $Y$ if the probability distribution function of $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-y},\\, & y\\ge 0, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186162651 "Let $X$ be a random variable with the probability density function shown below. What are the modes of $X?$\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{15}{128}(4{x}^{2}-{x}^{4}), & -2\\le x\\le 2 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186166510 "![](images/3f/3fa8db8d4186569020d5805ac76eee4f92f1c261dd2acb2502409a9b2b6db56b.svg)\nConsider the initial value problem above. By applying the Laplace transform to both sides of each equation, we get the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}\\boxed{\\text{[math]\\phantom{XXX}[/math]}}=-3{X}_{1}(s) \\\\ \\boxed{\\text{[math]\\phantom{XXX}[/math]}}=\\boxed{\\text{[math]\\phantom{XXXXXXXX}[/math]}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X_1(s) = \\mathcal{L}\\left\\{  x_1(t) \\right\\}$ and $X_2(s) = \\mathcal{L}\\left\\{  x_2(t) \\right\\}$ are the Laplace transforms of the functions $x_1(t)$ and $x_2(t),$ respectively."] [#edb/ref 17592186166534 "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=3x(t)-y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-2x(t)-y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. Find equations of the $x$- and $y$-nullclines for the corresponding phase space."] [#edb/ref 17592186166536 "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=4x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-6y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could represent the phase portrait of the decoupled system above?\n\n![](images/04/040ac76a28687db1c99d3379242b726a85aa4baf269a7c6d35d9662af1d3bd78.png)"] [#edb/ref 17592186166537 "![](images/25/25d4f77a3f596202229581e06d88a9e3bdd84dc6b8b7e14b6f46b3b3d2f125d2.png)\n\nConsider the phase portrait for a decoupled system above.\nWhich of the following systems could correspond to the given portrait?\n\nI. $\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-5x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=6y(t)\\end{aligned}$\n\nII. $\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=5x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=6y(t)\\end{aligned}$\n\nIII. $\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=5x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-6y(t)\\end{aligned}$\n\nIV. $\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-5x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-6y(t)\\end{aligned}$"] [#edb/ref 17592186166540 "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=2x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=4y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nInterpret the stability of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the equilibrium point (the origin) for the given decoupled system of linear differential equations."] [#edb/ref 17592186166542 "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-4y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could represent the phase portrait of the decoupled system above?\n\n![](images/47/470210fb9509e87c3e51a990bac7483e5862d2f37afdce9823279838e8391592.png)"] [#edb/ref 17592186166545 "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-8x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-8y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSketch the phase portrait of the linear system above."] [#edb/ref 17592186166546 "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-x(t)+y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-x(t)-3y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvector $\\mathbf{v} = [1, \\ -1]^T$ corresponds to the repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda=-2$ of the system's matrix $A.$ The vector $\\mathbf{w} = [0, \\ 1]^T$ is such that $(A - \\lambda I)\\mathbf{w}= \\mathbf{v}.$ Sketch the phase portrait of the linear system above."] [#edb/ref 17592186166547 "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=6x(t)+y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-x(t)+4y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above, where the system's matrix has repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda=5$ of multiplicity two. Determine the type and stability of the equilibrium point for this linear system."] [#edb/ref 17592186166550 "Consider the system of linear differential equations below.\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=9x(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe eigenvalues of the system's matrix are $\\lambda_{1,2} = \\pm3\\text{i},$ for which the corresponding eigenvectors are $\\mathbf{v}_{1,2} = [\\pm \\text{i}, 3]^T,$ respectively. Given that the general solution of the system is\n![](images/2b/2bc77eafe7c4362a1020c1f547a19ef0e72c309ebb82d1480e2ffec5bdfd8b38.svg)\nsketch the phase portrait of the system of differential equations given above."] [#edb/ref 17592186166552 "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-2y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=2x(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvalues of the system's matrix are $\\lambda=\\pm2\\text{i}.$ Provide an interpretation of the equilibrium point (origin) for the system of differential equations given above."] [#edb/ref 17592186166554 "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=-4x+7xy \\\\ {y}^{′}=9y-5xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the Lotka-Volterra model above, where $x=x(t)$ represents the predators and $y=y(t)$ represents the prey. Find the nonzero equilibrium of the model."] [#edb/ref 17592186166555 "Consider the Lotka-Volterra model above, where $x=x(t)$ represents the predators and $y=y(t)$ represents the prey.\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=-0.4x+0.4xy \\\\ {y}^{′}=y-4xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluate the Jacobian of the system at the equilibrium point $\\mathbf{x^\\ast}=[0.25, \\, 1]^T,$ and classify this equilibrium point."] [#edb/ref 17592186166556 "Consider the phase portrait above showing a solution of a Lotka-Volterra predator-prey model\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=-10x+5xy \\\\ {y}^{′}=21y-7xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $x=x(t), y=y(t),$ and $a,b,c,$ and $d$ are positive constants.\n\nDescribe the trajectory and the dynamics of the system from point $A$ to point $B$ on the solution.\n\n![](images/86/86002a25d2c60e07c34976541a1c050e606d20e4ffa7928a44f940bf5fa736e3.png)"] [#edb/ref 17592186166557 "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=-8x+2xy \\\\ {y}^{′}=4y-xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the Lotka-Volterra model above for the functions $x(t)$ and $y(t).$ Provide an interpretation of each equation in the model."] [#edb/ref 17592186166558 "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=5x(1-0.2x)-4xy \\\\ {y}^{′}=-6y+3xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the Lotka-Volterra model with carrying capacity above, where $x=x(t)$ represents the prey and $y=y(t)$ represents the predators. Find the equilibria of the model."] [#edb/ref 17592186166559 "Consider the Lotka-Volterra model with carrying capacity below, where $x=x(t)$ represents the prey and $y=y(t)$ represents the predators.\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=5x(1-x)-xy \\\\ {y}^{′}=-y+2xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluate the Jacobian of the system at the equilibrium point $\\mathbf{x^\\ast}=[0.5, \\, 2.5]^T,$ and classify this equilibrium point."] [#edb/ref 17592186166560 "Consider the Lotka-Volterra model with carrying capacity below, where $x=x(t)$ represents the prey and $y=y(t)$ represents the predators.\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=4x(1-2x)-3xy \\\\ {y}^{′}=-5y+4xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluate the Jacobian of the system at the equilibrium point $\\mathbf{x^\\ast}=[0.5, \\, 0]^T,$ and classify this equilibrium point."] [#edb/ref 17592186166561 "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=6x(1-0.1x)-3xy \\\\ {y}^{′}=-7y+5xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the modified Lotka-Volterra model, where $x=x(t)$ represents the prey and $y=y(t)$ represents the predators. Find the maximum carrying capacity for the prey in the case of the complete absence of predators."] [#edb/ref 17592186167159 "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)={x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=-{x}_{1}(t)+3{x}_{2}(t),\\end{aligned}\\,x(0)=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the initial value problem above. By applying the Laplace transform $L$ to both sides of each equation in our system, we get a system with variables ${X}_{1}(s)=L\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}$ and ${X}_{2}(s)=L\\{{x}_{2}(t)\\}.$ In terms of the parameter $s,