upled system above?"] [#edb/ref 17592186167215 "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=4y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could represent the phase portrait of the decoupled system above?"] [#edb/ref 17592186167230 "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=x(t)-4y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=9x(t)+y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvalues of the system's matrix are $\\lambda =1\\pm 6\\text{i}.$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\n$\\,$\nSince \n{{field-1}},\nthe equilibrium point (origin) is {{field-2}}."] [#edb/ref 17592186167233 "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)={x}_{1}(t)+4{x}_{2}(t)+9 \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=3{x}_{2}(t)+6,\\end{aligned}\\,x(0)=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the initial value problem above. By applying the Laplace transform to both sides of each equation in our system, we get a transformed system with the solution \n\n$$\n{X}_{1}(s)=L\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}=\\frac{1}{s-1}+\\frac{2}{s-3}-\\frac{1}{s},\\,{X}_{2}(s)=L\\{{x}_{2}(t)\\}=\\frac{1}{s-3}-\\frac{2}{s}.\n$$\n\nFind the solution to the original initial value problem.\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\" rowspan=\"2\">$x(t)=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td height=\"35px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>"] [#edb/ref 17592186167237 "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=5x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=5y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could represent the phase portrait of the decoupled system above?"] [#edb/ref 17592186167240 "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. Select the correct options below regarding the given system.\n\n$\\,$ The equilibrium point (origin) is a \n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}\nnode."] [#edb/ref 17592186167245 "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=2x-3xy \\\\ {y}^{′}=-5y+4xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the Lotka-Volterra model above, where $x=x(t)$ represents the prey and $y=y(t)$ represents the predators. Find the nonzero equilibrium of the model.\n\n$(${{field-1}}\n$,$\n{{field-2}}$)$"] [#edb/ref 17592186167248 "![](images/71/718df02f2d0c2f01cc4a02a40da120178afe0f436bbf38cd5a8d249d87b5d1c9.png)\n\nConsider the phase portrait above showing a solution of a Lotka-Volterra predator-prey model\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=2x-xy \\\\ {y}^{′}=-6y+2xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $x=x(t),y=y(t),$ and $a,b,c,$ and $d$ are positive constants.\n\nSelect the options below that best describe the dynamics of the system from point $A$ to point $B$ on the solution.\n\n$\\,$\nThe trajectory \n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$\nThe number of prey {{field-3}} and the number of predators {{field-4}}."] [#edb/ref 17592186167250 "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=-4x+2xy \\\\ {y}^{′}=6y-2xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the Lotka-Volterra model above for the functions $x(t)$ and $y(t).$ Select the correct options in the following statement.\n\n$\\,$\nTerm ${y}^{′}$ in the second equation represents the \n{{field-1}} \nof the \n{{field-2}}."] [#edb/ref 17592186167256 "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=3x-2xy \\\\ {y}^{′}=-4y+5xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the Lotka-Volterra model above, where $x=x(t)$ represents the prey and $y=y(t)$ represents the predators. Find the nonzero equilibrium of the model.\n\n$(${{field-1}}\n$,$\n{{field-2}}$)$"] [#edb/ref 17592186167260 "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=2x(1-0.2x)-xy \\\\ {y}^{′}=-4y+2xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the Lotka-Volterra model with carrying capacity above, where $x=x(t)$ represents the prey and $y=y(t)$ represents the predators. The equilibria of the model are the following:\n\n$(0,0),\\,(${{field-1}}$,0),\\,(${{field-2}}$,${{field-3}}$)$"] [#edb/ref 17592186167261 "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=8x(1-0.75x)-xy \\\\ {y}^{′}=-4y+4xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the Lotka-Volterra model with carrying capacity above, where $x=x(t)$ represents the prey and $y=y(t)$ represents the predators. Select the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\n$\\,$ Evaluating the Jacobian of the system at the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=[1,\\,2]^{T},$ we get\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$J({x}^{∗})=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"40px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"40px\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$\\,.$</td>\n</tr><tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-3}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-4}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\n$\\,$ The eigenvalues of the Jacobian matrix are \n{{field-5}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the equilibrium at ${x}^{∗}$ is \n{{field-6}}{{field-7}}."] [#edb/ref 17592186167292 "$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}, & x\\in [-3,0] \\\\ -{x}^{2}, & x\\in (0,3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the function $F(x)$ shown above. Find the value to which the Fourier series of the $6$-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is converging when $x=2.$\n\nThe Fourier series converges to {{field-1}} when $x=2.$"] [#edb/ref 17592186167293 "$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & x\\in [-\\pi ,0] \\\\ 1, & x\\in (0,\\pi )\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the function $F(x)$ shown above. Find the value to which the Fourier series of the $2\\pi $-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ converges when $x=\\pi .$\n\nThe Fourier series converges to {{field-1}} when $x=\\pi .$"] [#edb/ref 17592186167294 "$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & x\\in [-\\pi ,0] \\\\ 1, & x\\in (0,\\pi )\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the function $F(x)$ shown above. The Fourier series of the $2\\pi $-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{1}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{\\pi (2n-1)}\\operatorname{sin}⁡((2n-1)x).\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\nAt $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡((2n-1)\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2})={{field-1}}.\n$$\n\nSince $f(x)$ is \n{{field-2}}\nat $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ the corresponding Fourier series converges to \n\n$$\nf(\\frac{\\pi }{2})={{field-3}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have \n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{2n-1}={{field-4}}.\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186171717 "Suppose we want to find the minimum of the function $f(x,y,z)=2xz+y^2$ on the intersection of the surfaces $x-z=2$ and $x^2+y^2+2z^2=3$ using the method of Lagrange multipliers. Then, the corresponding Lagrange multipliers $\\lambda$ and $\\mu$ satisfy the following system: \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}⟨2z,\\,\\bbox[2pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AA}},\\,2x⟩=\\lambda ⟨1,\\,0,\\,\\bbox[2pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AA}}\\,⟩+\\mu ⟨2x,\\,2y,\\,\\bbox[2pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AA}}\\,⟩ \\\\ x-z=2 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+2{z}^{2}=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom left to right, what are the missing parts of the expressions?"] [#edb/ref 17592186172383 "Suppose we would like to find the maximum of the function $f(x,y,z)=x{y}^{2}z$ on the intersection of the surfaces ${x}^{2}-2{z}^{2}=1$ and ${y}^{2}+3{z}^{3}=5$ using the method of Lagrange multipliers. Then, the corresponding Lagrange multipliers $\\lambda $ and $\\mu $ satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}⟨{y}^{2}z,\\,\\bbox[2pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AA}},\\,x{y}^{2}⟩=\\lambda ⟨2x,\\,0,\\,\\bbox[2pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AA}}\\,⟩+\\mu ⟨0,\\,2y,\\,\\bbox[2pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AA}}\\,⟩ \\\\ {x}^{2}-2{z}^{2}=1 \\\\ {y}^{2}+3{z}^{3}=5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom left to right, what are the missing parts of the expressions?"] [#edb/ref 17592186172384 "Suppose we would like to find the minimum value of the function $f(x,y,z)=x+{y}^{2}+z$ on the intersection of the surfaces $3{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=2$ and $x+{z}^{2}=1$ using the method of Lagrange multipliers. Then, the corresponding Lagrange multipliers $\\lambda $ and $\\mu $ satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}⟨1,\\,2y,\\,1⟩=\\lambda ⟨0,\\,\\bbox[2pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AA}},\\,2z⟩+\\mu ⟨1,\\,\\bbox[2pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AA}},\\,2z⟩ \\\\ 3{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=2 \\\\ x+{z}^{2}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom left to right, what are the missing expressions?"] [#edb/ref 17592186176258 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{8}{3}{x}^{3}y, & 0\\le x\\le 1,\\,1\\le y\\le 2, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the expression for the marginal PDF ${f}_{Y}(y).$"] [#edb/ref 17592186176261 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}), & 0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind $P(0.2<X<1).$"] [#edb/ref 17592186176262 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}4xy, & 0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe marginal CDF ${F}_{X}(x)$ of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\n{F}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & x<0 \\\\ g(x), & 0\\le x\\le 1 \\\\ 1 & x>1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhat is the function $g(x)?$"] [#edb/ref 17592186176276 "![](images/c6/c64cfa701997c2b9e5e54f7eb77afb9927b878065af9c32fed86f09f140a4ec4.png)\n\nLet $X$ and $Y$ be continuous random variables with the joint probability density function \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}x+y, & (x,y)\\in D, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf $F(x,y)$ is the joint CDF of $X$ and $Y,$ find $F(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{5}).$"] [#edb/ref 17592186176373 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be continuous random variables with the joint probability density function \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{2}{y}^{2}, & 0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 3, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the expression for the corresponding joint CDF when $x>1,\\,y>3.$"] [#edb/ref 17592186176374 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be continuous random variables with the joint probability density function \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{2}{y}^{2}, & 0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 3, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the expression for the corresponding joint CDF when $x<0,\\,1<y<3.$"] [#edb/ref 17592186176376 "A random variable $X$ has the probability distribution given below.\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{10}, & x=0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}, & x=1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{5}, & x=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculate $\\text{E}[{X}^{4}].$"] [#edb/ref 17592186184775 "Given the vector $x$ and the system of linear equations below, find the projection of $x$ onto the solution space of the system.\n\n$$\nx=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 14\\end{bmatrix},\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-3{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}-9{x}_{2}+6{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186188538 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{2}y, & 0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 2, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that $\\text{E}[X]=\\frac{3}{4},$ find $\\text{E}[4X+6Y].$"] [#edb/ref 17592186188546 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables whose marginal density functions are as follows:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}, & \\,0\\le x\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}4{y}^{3}, & \\,0\\le y\\le 1 