ute $\\text{Var}[X].$\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{9}{4{x}^{3}},\\, & 1<x<3, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186484298 "The continuous random variable $X$ has the probability density function $f(x)$ stated below. Given that $\\text{E}[X]=4$ compute $\\text{Var}[X].$ \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2x}{27}, & 0<x<3, \\\\ \\frac{9-x}{27}, & 3\\le x<9, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186486383 "Find $\\text{E}[\\frac{1}{XY}],$ given that random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{4}{x}^{2}{y}^{3},\\, & 0<x<1,\\,0<y<2, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186486393 "Suppose we have a random sample of size $n=140$ from a population where $60%$ of the population has a particular characteristic. Let the random variable ${X}_{i}$ be equal to\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=\\{\\begin{aligned}1,\\,i\\text{th member of the sample has the characteristic} \\\\ 0,\\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $i=1,…,140.$ If the statistic $X$ is defined as\n\n$$\nX={\\sum }_{i=1}^{140}{X}_{i}\n$$\n\nfind approximate values of $\\text{E}[X]$ and $\\text{Var}[X].$\n\n*Hint: You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.*"] [#edb/ref 17592186486412 "Find $\\text{E}[X+3{Y}^{2}],$ given that random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{15},\\, & 0\\le x\\le 3,\\,\\frac{x}{3}\\le y\\le 3; \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186486430 "Find $\\text{E}[XY],$ given that random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3xy},\\, & 1\\le x\\le y,\\,e\\le y\\le {e}^{2}, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186486435 "Suppose we have a random sample of size $n=66$ from a population where $42%$ of t